production requires substantial resources such as land, water, and energy, placing additional pressure on the environment (FAO, 2021). Unused organic waste has high potential for conversion into biogas, compost, or organic fertilizers, emphasizing its value as a resource (Cherubini, 2010).
Finding effective methods for organic waste utilization has become a top priority amid a growing environmental crisis. One promising solution is bioconversion using black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens), which not only significantly reduces waste volume but also produces valuable outputs, such as feed protein and organic fertilizers (Van Huis, 2013). This approach helps reduce landfill waste, produce sustainable alternatives to conventional feed, and lower the agroindustrial chain’s carbon footprint (Gold et al., 2021). The technology has been successfully implemented in various countries, and its scaling and automation have contributed to the widespread adoption of this model. The black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) represent a promising tool for organic waste bioconversion. This insect species is characterized by rapid growth, low maintenance requirements, and the ability to process a wide range of organic materials—from food and agricultural waste to manure and residues from the food processing industry—efficiently.
The bioconversion process involving H. illucens larvae is based on their natural ability to rapidly ingest and transform organic matter into valuable biomass rich in proteins, fats, and micronutrients. Simultaneously, the overall volume of waste is significantly reduced, the microbiological load is decreased, and environmental pollution is minimized. Compared with traditional waste disposal methods (such as composting, incineration, and landfilling), the use of black soldier fly larvae offers several significant advantages. Research indicates that H. illucens larvae can reduce the mass of organic waste by 50%–80% within 10–14 days, while the substrate-to-larval biomass conversion rate (bioconversion rate) ranges from 15% to 25%, depending on the rearing conditions (Diener et al., 2009; Surendra et al., 2016). Thus, H. illucens represents an environmentally friendly and highly efficient biological agent capable of playing a key role in the development of sustainable organic waste management systems and the provision of alternative sources of feed protein (Macwan et al., 2024). This article reviews the environmental and health impacts of organic waste accumulation and explores innovative processing methods using larvae of the black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens). This study aims to systematically analyze the research and applications of larvae as bioconversion agents for transforming organic waste into high-value feed. This review examines the biological characteristics, rearing technologies, nutritional composition, safety, and regulatory frameworks of larvae-derived products. Environmental
and economic benefits, such as waste reduction, resource efficiency, and greenhouse gas mitigation, are also evaluated. Finally, the study identifies knowledge gaps and outlines strategic directions for the further development of this sustainable technology in agriculture and the circular bioeconomy.
Hermetia illucens: Biology, Life Cycle, and Nutritional Value
Hermetia illucens: life cycle and ecological role
The black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens), a member of the Stratiomyidae family, is a dipteran insect species that plays a significant role in sustainable development due to its capacity to process organic waste and produce valuable products such as protein-rich biomass and biofertilizer (Diclaro and Kaufman, 2025). This species has attracted considerable attention in agriculture and the circular economy because its larvae efficiently convert food scraps, manure, and agricultural byproducts into animal feed and fertilizer resources (Sheppard et al., 1994; Van Huis, 2013).
Hermetia illucens stands out among insects because of its unique characteristics. Adult flies do not feed and are not vectors of pathogens, making them safe for use in agricultural systems (Wang and Shelomi, 2017). The primary value of this insect lies in its larvae, which can process up to 75% of organic waste, reduce its mass by 60%–72%, and transform it into a biomass rich in proteins (35%–40%) and fats (15%–25%) (Surendra et al., 2016; Liu et al., 2018). This makes the larvae a promising feed source for poultry, fish, and pigs, particularly in the context of the global protein shortage (Makkar et al., 2014).
The ecological role of H. illucens is multifaceted. The larvae significantly reduce the volume of organic waste and help lower methane and other greenhouse gas emissions (Diener et al., 2009). Composting organic waste with BSF larvae resulted in significant nutrient enrichment, with total phosphorus increasing up to 32.4% (compared to 10.3% in the control) and total potassium reaching a maximum mean increase of 77.1%, thereby emphasizing the strong fertilizing potential of the final compost (Sarpong et. al., 2019). Moreover, the larvae contribute to pathogen reduction by inactivating harmful bacteria such as Salmonella spp. and Escherichia coli (with a 99.9% reduction), thanks to their stable gut microbiota (Lalander et al., 2013). They can also degrade microplastics, reducing the mass of polystyrene by 30%–40% within 30 days (Yang et al., 2023). From an economic perspective, the species is highly attractive: its larvae are used to produce protein meal and oil, which can substitute conventional feed ingredients such as soybean meal or fishmeal (Spranghers et al., 2017). The life cycle of H. illucens consists of four stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. Each stage has its own


