Egg stage: Female Hermetia illucens lay between 500 and 900 eggs in dry, protected areas near organic substrates. Incubation lasted 4–6 days at 25°C–30°C and relative humidity of 60%–80% (Tomberlin et al., 2002). Extreme environmental conditions (temperatures 20°C or >40°C) significantly reduce egg viability (Chia et al., 2018).
Larval Stage: This critical phase for bioconversion involves 5–6 instars over 7–14 days (up to 20 days under certain conditions), during which larvae reach a dry mass of approximately 43.5–44.0 mg. By day 14, the first prepupae appeared on all tested substrates, and by day 19, more than half had emerged, generally exceeding 210 mg per larva, except on poultry manure (165 mg), with the largest prepupae, averaging just
over 250 mg, developing on abattoir waste combined with fruits and vegetables (Lalander et al., 2019). BSF larvae have a highly efficient digestive system that enables them to break down organic waste into valuable byproducts, such as protein-rich biomass and fertilizer. Their gut is equipped with digestive enzymes to efficiently process various organic materials, including amylase for carbohydrates, proteases for proteins, and lipases for lipids. They possess a highly efficient digestive system enriched with enzymesamylases (85 U/g) and proteases (~100 U/g)—capable of breaking down carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids (Wang and Shelomi, 2017). Optimal rearing conditions include a temperature of 27°C–30°C, substrate moisture of 60%–80%, and larval density of 2.5–5.0 larvae/cm2. Larvae process food waste, manure, and agricultural byproducts into high-value biomass containing 40%–45.2% protein and 20%–35% lipids, including antimicrobial lauric acid (Spranghers et al., 2017).
High fiber content (>7%) slows larval growth, whereas substrate pretreatment (e.g., fermentation, grinding) can enhance bioconversion efficiency by 10%–15% (Liu et al., 2018). Pupal stage: At this stage, the larvae stop feeding and begin to pupate. The adult fly emerges from the pupa within 7–14 days. Environmental factors, such as temperature and humidity, influence the duration of this stage (Chia et al., 2018). Adult stage: Adult flies live for 5–8 days, do not feed, and focus exclusively on reproduction. They do not pose a threat as pests because they do not interact with food or crops (Sheppard et al., 2002). Hermetia illucens makes a significant contribution to the circular economy and sustainable agriculture. Its ability to process organic waste reduces the burden on landfills and mitigates greenhouse gas emissions,
