Functional characteristics of H. illucens Larvae in bioconversion processes
Hermetia illucens larvae can convert up to 50%–80% of the mass of organic waste—including food residues, pig manure, and agricultural by-products—within 2–4 weeks. Sheppard et al. (2002) highlighted their ability to effectively degrade pig manure, significantly reducing both its volume and environmental impact. According to Bruno et al. (2025), the powerful digestive system of larvae, rich in enzymes such as lipases and proteases, enables them to break down complex organic substrates. The larvae contain 40%50% protein and 20%–35% fat on a dry matter basis, making them a valuable raw material for animal feed (Lu et al., 2024).
Tahamtani et al. (2021) demonstrated that the inclusion of live larvae in the diets of laying hens had no negative effects on bird health or behavior and even increased feed palatability, confirming their nutritional value. Thongpea et al. (2022) further emphasized that larval biomass is rich in essential amino acids (e.g., lysine and methionine) and fatty acids (notably lauric acid), making it suitable for use in both feed and biofuel production. Larvae convert low-value substrates into nutrient-rich biomass (Rehman et al., 2022). Bruno et al. (2025) emphasized that the larval digestive system—supported by symbiotic microorganisms—efficiently decomposes organic matter while accumulating proteins and lipids. Sheppard et al. (2002) noted that larvae reared on pig manure produce biomass suitable for feed, and Mou
et al. (2024) added that such biomass is already being used in aquaculture and livestock farming. According to Ogello et al. (2025) and Alagappan et al. (2025), the highest protein content was observed in larvae reared on mixed feed (49.2%), while the lowest was found in those fed on poultry manure (39.1%). This indicates that the substrate type has a significant impact on the larval nutritional profile (Fig. 2).
Hermetia illucens larvae develop from egg to prepupa within 14–28 days under optimal conditions (27°C–30°C temperature, 60%–70% humidity), ensuring high productivity. Sheppard et al. (2002) described cultivation methods that optimize this cycle, including the use of containers with controlled humidity. Bruno et al. (2025) and Tomberlin et al. (2015) reported that the physiological adaptations of larvae enhance growth on various substrates. These larvae are highly tolerant to a broad range of substrates and environmental conditions (15°C–40°C and varying humidity levels). Bruno et al. (2025) explained that the digestive system of larvae adapts to diverse waste types due to a wide array of enzymes and gut microbiota (Nguyen et al., 2015). Alagappan et al. (2025) and Park et al. (2015) emphasized that this property makes bioconversion products safe for use in animal feed.
Bioconversion using BSF larvae significantly reduces landfill waste volume and greenhouse gas emissions. Čičková et al. (2015) added that larval frass (excrement) is a valuable organic fertilizer rich in nitrogen and phosphorus, contributing to sustainable agriculture. Hermetia illucens larvae can process food waste, manure, grain residues, and even certain plastics (Fig. 3). While Sheppard et al. (2002) focused on pig manure, Bruno et al. (2025) highlighted the digestive capacity of larvae to handle complex substrates, including kitchen waste and industrial by-products. Yang et al. (2023) and their potential in aquaculture waste management.
