Bioconversion of BSF larvae requires minimal infrastructure. Sheppard et al. (2002) described simple container-based cultivation methods, while Alagappan et al. (2025) (2024) emphasized the cost-effectiveness of this technology, making it suitable for both smalland large-scale operations. Larval biomass is used in animal feed, biofuels, cosmetics, and pharmaceuticals, while frass serves as a sustainable fertilizer. Tahamtani et al. (2021) confirmed that live larvae improve the diet of laying hens without affecting egg quality. Wang and Shelomi (2017) emphasized the high commercial potential of pig manure bioconversion products. Comparative nutritional profile of feed insects
Insect larvae are a rich source of protein, with content varying depending on the insect species, developmental stage, and nutritional substrate (Table 2). According to various studies, the nutritional composition of H. illucens larvae fluctuates based on diet, developmental stage, and rearing conditions. According to research, the crude protein content is as follows: black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) larvae contain 42%–50% protein on a dry matter basis, increasing up to 65% after fat extraction (Nekrasov et al., 2022); green bottle fly (Lucilia sericata) larvae can contain up to 60% protein when reared on optimized substrates, such as mixtures including camelina oil and mealworm (Tenebrio molitor) larvae, which contain approximately 47%–53% protein (Oonincx
and de Boer, 2012). Based on the studies by Nekrasov et al. (2019), Finke (2002), and Ewald et al. (2020), Table 3 summarizes the nutritional composition of three commonly used insect larvae species in animal feed: black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens), mealworm (Tenebrio molitor), and green bottle fly (Lucilia sericata).
The protein composition of insect larvae includes a wide range of essential amino acids—such as lysine, tryptophan, and methionine—whose profile is comparable to soybean meal and fishmeal, making insect larvae a promising alternative to conventional feed ingredients (Al-Qazzaz and Ismail, 2016). For example, dipteran larvae have higher lysine and tryptophan contents than crickets or locusts. Hermetia illucens larvae provide a valuable source of essential amino acids (Table 4; Makkar et al., 2014).
Insect larvae also contain significant amounts of lipids, ranging from 15% to 40% of dry matter, with black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) larvae containing up to 35% fat. Notably, their lipid profile includes high concentrations of lauric acid (up to 60% of total fat), which possesses strong antimicrobial properties (Ewald et al., 2020) (Table 5). Lauric acid and specific antimicrobial peptides (e.g., cecropins and sarcotoxins) found in larvae have been shown to reduce pathogenic microbial load, thus potentially lowering the need for antibiotics in animal production (Borrelli et al., 2021).
