Larval fats provide a high energy value of 18–22 MJ/kg and can be tailored to the nutritional needs of specific animal species by modifying the larval substrate. Insect larvae also supply important macro- and microelements, including calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, zinc, and B vitamins. Black soldier fly larvae contain up to 5%–8% calcium and 1%–2% phosphorus in their dry matter, which is higher than that in many conventional feed ingredients (Chobanova et al., 2024).
The carbohydrate content in H. illucens larvae is relatively low, around 5%–10% of dry matter, and the chitin fraction accounts for approximately 4%–7% (ranging from 3.9% to 7.2%), which aligns with the estimated 5%–8% chitin as part of the total carbohydrate component in the larvae (Wang et al., 2020; Lu et al., 2022). The energy value of insect larvae—18–22 MJ/kg of dry matter—is comparable to that of concentrated feed sources such as fishmeal, and their digestibility is high, reaching up to 95% in poultry and fish, making them an efficient feed component (Nekrasov et al., 2019).
Inclusion of insect larvae in animal feed improves growth performance and health status. For example,
broiler chickens (Ross 308) fed with diets containing insect protein showed improved feed conversion and reduced pathogenic bacterial load. The inclusion of black soldier fly larvae also enhanced growth rates and positively influenced the gut microbiota (Nekrasov et al., 2019). Despite their high nutritional value, the use of insect larvae in feed has certain limitations. Until 2021, several countries had restricted the use of insects in livestock feed. Furthermore, the potential accumulation of toxic elements (Ba, Hg, and Mo) in larvae necessitates strict substrate control (Bosch et al., 2014). Negative consumer perception regarding the use of insects in animal feed may also limit their adoption.
