less than 60 kg remains, and approximately 50 kg remains by day 14 (Dewi et al., 2024). This demonstrates the larvae’s ability to reduce more than 50% of the organic material, significantly decreasing waste volume and contributing to environmentally sustainable waste management (Fitriana et al., 2021). Inoculation of H. illucens larvae and initial growth
Inoculation is typically performed using larvae that are 3–5 days old. On average, between 5,000 and 10,000 larvae are introduced per 1 kg of substrate (Okpoko et al., 2024). The larvae quickly penetrate the substrate, thereby initiating the consumption of organic matter and subsequent growth (Diener, 2009). Optimal development occurs under the following conditions: temperature of 27°C–30°C, relative humidity of 60%80% (Table 6), and darkness (Wang and Shelomi, 2017). The average feeding phase lasts between 7 and 14 days, during which the larval mass can increase by a factor of 1,000–1,500 (Barragán-Fonseca et al., 2017). This stage is characterized by the intensive degradation of organic matter and the production of a by-product known as frass, which can be used as an organic fertilizer. Figure 5 illustrates the exponential increase in the mass of H. illucens larvae during a 14-day growth period. The larval weight increased from 0.01 to 7.0 g, confirming their high productivity under optimal conditions (temperature: 27°C–30°C; humidity: 60%–70%) (Huang et al., 2025). The most intensive weight gain occurred between days 5 and 11, after which the growth rate stabilized. This dynamic allows for accurate scheduling of larval harvest to maximize biomass yield (Diener et al., 2009).
