Feed efficiency and applications of Hermetia illucensbased products
The resulting protein meal and fats can be used as components in compound feeds for broilers, laying hens, pigs, fish, shrimp, and even pets. The larvae supply animals with high-quality animal protein, B-group vitamins, minerals (Ca, P, Mg), and fatty acidsincluding lauric acid (C12:0), which has pronounced antimicrobial activity. The use of H. illucens-based feed allows the following: to replace up to 100% of fish meal in aquaculture diets (Henry et al., 2015); improve feed conversion ratios; reduce CO2 emissions and
resource consumption. Bioconversion of organic waste using H. illucens larvae is a sustainable, economically viable, and environmentally friendly technology. It allows for efficient biomass recycling, waste volume reduction, and high-quality feed ingredient production suitable for industrial-scale use (Table 7).
Organic resources for bioconversion: suitable substrates for Hermetia illucens
Bioconversion of organic waste using larvae of H. illucens (black soldier fly) is becoming an increasingly popular technology for sustainable biomass management and feed production. The process efficiency largely depends on the quality and composition of the initial raw materials. Food waste is one of the most common and accessible types of raw materials for bioconversion. It includes food scraps from restaurants, cafeterias, and supermarkets, as well as vegetable and fruit peels (Diener et al., 2011; Van Huis et al., 2013). Food waste is characterized by high moisture and nutrient content, which promotes rapid growth of larvae. Agricultural residues—such as straw, crop tops, grain processing byproducts, and vegetable and fruit peels—are also used in bioconversion (Makkar et al., 2014; Surendra et al., 2016). To enhance nutrient accessibility, these materials often require pretreatment (e.g., grinding or moistening).
