Animal-derived waste can be used provided that it strictly complies with sanitary regulations. This includes meat and fish byproducts, poultry manure, and other farm waste (Nguyen et al., 2015; Jucker et al., 2017). However, such waste requires careful monitoring due to the risk of pathogenic microorganisms.
Household food waste and industrial food processing byproducts, including dairy residues and brewery waste, are also recognized as promising raw materials for bioconversion (Li et al., 2011; Lalander et al., 2019). Conversely, certain types of waste, such as inorganic materials, toxic, or chemically contaminated substrates, are unsuitable for bioconversion by larvae due to the risk of contaminating the final product and reducing processing efficiency (Gold et al., 2020).
Thus, selecting the optimal type of organic waste is a critical factor for the successful application of H. illucens larvae bioconversion technology to produce feed and reduce environmental impact.
Factors affecting the efficiency of bioconversion of organic waste by Hermetia illucens Larvae
The efficiency of organic waste bioconversion using larvae of H. illucens (black soldier fly) depends on a
combination of biotechnological and environmental factors that determine larval growth rate, substrate utilization efficiency, and the quality of the final product (Surendra et al., 2016; Lalander et al., 2019). Temperature is a critical parameter that affects larval metabolism and development. The optimal temperature range for rearing H. illucens is approximately 27°C–30°C. Lower temperatures slow growth rates, whereas temperatures above 35°C cause stress and increased larval mortality (Diener et al., 2011; Nguyen et al., 2015). A stable temperature ensures maximum bioconversion efficiency and biomass quality.
Substrate moisture should be maintained within 60%70% to provide larvae with optimal feeding conditions. Substrates with low moisture content limit nutrient accessibility and reduce larval activity, whereas excessive moisture promotes pathogen growth and anaerobic processes (Jucker et al., 2017).
