Effective management of these factors significantly enhances the efficiency of bioconversion using H. illucens larvae, reducing waste volume, and producing high-quality feed material. Optimizing rearing parameters for each specific waste type is recommended to maximize productivity.
Global Approaches and Innovations in Bioconversion of Waste into Feed
Global spread of bioconversion technology involving H. illucens
Bioconversion technology using black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) has gained widespread adoption across many regions worldwide due to its high efficiency in processing various organic wastes. Infrastructure for specialized farms cultivating H. illucens larvae is currently developing in Europe, Asia, North America, and Africa. These enterprises create new value-added chains within the agro-industrial complex by combining waste management solutions with the production of high-quality feed ingredients (Li et al., 2011; Lalander et al., 2019). The main directions of technology implementation include the following:
- Food waste utilization: The technology enables significant reduction of biowaste volumes at food processing plants, supermarkets, and restaurants while simultaneously producing high-protein feedstock. This approach reduces landfill burdens and lowers GHG emissions (Makkar et al., 2014).
- Feed production for livestock and aquaculture: H. illucens larvae biomass is used as an ingredient in compound feeds for poultry, pigs, fish, and other animals, reducing dependence on traditional protein sources such as fishmeal and soy, with economic and environmental benefits (Jucker et al., 2017).
- The development of a circular economy. Organic waste bioconversion is integrated into closed
production loops where waste is converted into valuable resources. After larval processing, residues are used as organic fertilizers, promoting sustainable agriculture and reducing environmental pollution (Gold et al., 2020).
- Scientific and technical innovations. The current research focuses on optimizing larval rearing conditions, improving feed product quality, increasing bioconversion efficiency, and scaling production processes. Simultaneously, regulatory authorities in several countries are developing frameworks to ensure the safety and standardization of bioconversion products used in feed production (EFSA, 2015, 2021).
Overall, the adoption of H. illucens larvae in organic waste management and feed production systems reflects a global trend toward sustainable development. This technology addresses critical challenges related to food security and environmental responsibility by combining innovative biotechnology with the principles of circular economy.
Waste bioconversion using Hermetia illucens Larvae in different regions of the world
The development of bioconversion technology using black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) larvae is advancing rapidly across various continents due to its high ecological and economic efficiency (Opoku et al., 2023). The successful integration of this technology into waste management systems and feed production is illustrated in the following examples.
European Union (Netherlands, France, and Germany). The Dutch company Protix is considered one of the global leaders in insect-based protein production. It has successfully implemented H. illucens larvae rearing technologies for processing food waste and produces feed lines for pets, fish, and poultry. Their facility processes thousands of tons of organic waste annually, simultaneously producing high-quality protein and organic fertilizers (Van Huis et al., 2013; Gold et al., 2018).
Ynsect, a French company, is also developing large industrial complexes focused on producing insectbased feed protein and fertilizers, investing heavily in research and sustainable technologies.
Asia (China, South Korea, and Vietnam). In China, a farm in Guangdong Province processes over 50 tons of food and kitchen waste daily using H. illucens, producing both protein feed and fat for use in pig and poultry diets (Yu et al., 2020).
According to Nguyen et al. (2015), the use of H. illucens larvae has become an important solution for agricultural waste management in Vietnam and provides local farmers with sustainable feed alternatives. As part of a national strategy to reduce biowaste in urban and suburban areas, South Korea is promoting government initiatives to implement insect-based technologies (Surendra et al., 2016).


