Africa (Kenya, Uganda, and South Africa). The technology has gained widespread popularity in Kenya, Uganda, and other African countries due to its accessibility and ability to address feed shortages. The Kenyan startup InsectiPro collaborates with smallholder farmers to train them in the production of H. illucens larvae for use in poultry and fish feed, which contributes to food security and improves the profitability of rural farms (Lalander et al., 2019).
In South Africa, the company AgriProtein is a pioneer in commercial insect-based waste processing. Its facilities process up to 250 tons of organic waste per day, providing sustainable protein sources for the feed industry. Latin America (Brazil and Colombia). In Brazil, several projects focus on processing sugarcane and corn residues. Agricultural universities are conducting experiments to integrate H. illucens into organic waste recycling cycles. As part of a national sustainable agriculture strategy, Colombia actively employs bioconversion technologies to produce protein and organic fertilizers. North America (USA and Canada). The American company Enterra Feed Corporation (also operating in Canada) specializes in the production of protein feeds and fertilizers from H. illucens. Their facilities process food and agricultural waste, creating a circular production model that minimizes waste (Jucker et al., 2017).
In the United States, ongoing research is exploring the integration of insect-derived products into aquaculture feed chains to reduce pressure on wild fish stocks.
These examples from various regions demonstrate the high potential of waste bioconversion using H. illucens larvae as a tool for sustainable development. They confirm that integrating larvae into agri-food and environmental systems can yield both economic benefits and a significant reduction in ecological impact regardless of geography.
Industrial bioconversion of organic waste using Hermetia illucens
In recent years, significant technological advancements have emerged in the field of organic waste bioconversion, particularly aimed at the industrial scaling of processes involving the black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens). These innovations encompass the automation of production cycles and the enhancement of engineering solutions for managing microclimate, substrate, and insect development stages. The development of automated larval rearing systems, which include controlled modules for substrate delivery, ventilation, temperature, and humidity regulation, is a key technological breakthrough. For instance, “containerized Black Soldier Fly Larvae production units” enable the bioconversion cycle to be conducted in closed climate-controlled environments with a high level of biosecurity, allowing such facilities to be located even within urban areas (Pahmeyer et al., 2020).
In addition, smart monitoring and control platforms based on the internet of things and machine learning
have been introduced. These systems track larval behavior, substrate consumption, and growth rates in real time, enabling prompt environmental adjustments to improve productivity and reduce processing time (Avila et al., 2022). Another significant achievement is the optimization of logistics and engineering solutions for sorting and drying larval biomass, as well as for the disposal of residues (frass fractions). Modern installations can produce standardized protein and fat feed ingredients that comply with international safety standards (Van Huis, 2020; Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2021). Large-scale farms, such as Ynsect (France), Protix (Netherlands), and AgriProtein (South Africa), have become pioneers in the commercialization of bioconversion technologies and have demonstrated successful industrial-scale operations, processing tens of tons of waste daily (Byrne, 2024).
Thus, technological advancements in waste bioconversion using H. illucens are focused on creating highly efficient, sustainable, and safe solutions capable of providing alternative feed sources while simultaneously reducing environmental impact.
Regulatory framework and safety assurance for Hermetia illucens Larvae-derived feed products
With the advancement of waste bioconversion technologies using H. illucens larvae, compliance with regulatory requirements and product safety assurance have become critical factors for the sustainable integration of these solutions into the feed industry. Regulatory authorities worldwide are increasingly developing legal frameworks aimed at controlling substrates, processing conditions, and final product quality (Regulation (EU) 2017/893) (Table 8).
Use of black soldier fly Larvae (Hermetia illucens) as a feed component requires strict compliance with biological and chemical safety standards
The cultivation, processing, and storage of H. illucens larvae for use in animal feed must adhere to stringent safety protocols to ensure both biological and chemical safety. The following key factors should be considered: - Microbiological safety: Hygienic conditions during larval rearing and processing are crucial for preventing feed contamination with pathogenic microorganisms. Controlling pathogens such as Salmonella spp., Escherichia coli, Listeria monocytogenes, and other potentially harmful bacteria According to EFSA (2021a), maintaining thermal treatment conditions of at least 90 °C for 15 minutes effectively reduces microbiological risks.
- Chemical safety (Heavy Metals and Toxins): One of the critical concerns is the absorption of heavy metals (lead, cadmium, mercury) and mycotoxins from substrates. Studies have shown that the concentration of toxic substances in larvae can directly depend on the composition of the substrate used; therefore, prior chemical analysis is required for the application of organic waste (Van der Fels-Klerx et al., 2018).


