Residues of antibiotics and pharmaceuticals: When using food or agricultural waste, it is important to eliminate the presence of antibiotic residues, hormones, and pesticides, as they may accumulate in the larvae and enter the food chain. EU standards (Regulation (EC) No 767/2009) and AAFCO (USA) require the mandatory testing of residual substances. Allergens: Although allergic reactions to insects are rare, potential cross-reactivity with crustaceans and mites has been reported. Therefore, in Europe, H. illucens-derived feed products must be appropriately labeled (EFSA, 2021b).
Processing and preservation: To minimize risks and extend shelf life, larvae should undergo thermal processing (e.g., drying, boiling, roasting) and be stored under controlled conditions (humidity, temperature, pest exclusion) (EFSA, 2015).
Traceability and raw material control: Each batch of larvae and substrates used must be accompanied by documentation verifying the origin, rearing conditions, and laboratory test results, which is essential for supply chain traceability and quality control.
Research by EFSA (2021a, b), Schlüter et al. (2017), and Van der Fels-Klerx et al. (2018) confirmed that when rearing and sanitation standards are followed, H. illucens larvae represent a safe and reliable protein source for feed applications.
Feed Solutions Based on Hermetia illucens Larvae: Application and Effectiveness Areas
Use of H. illucens larvae in animal feed
In recent years, black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) larvae have been increasingly integrated into feed production as a promising source of high-quality protein and fat. Their ability to efficiently convert organic waste, combined with rapid growth and adaptability, makes them particularly suitable for use in poultry farming, aquaculture, and pig production (Henry et al., 2015; Barragán-Fonseca et al., 2017; Schiavone et al., 2017; Parodi et al., 2018; Elahi Usman et al.
2022). Larval feed contains up to 42%–45% crude protein and up to 35% fat, as well as antimicrobial and immunostimulatory compounds such as lauric acid and chitin (Mwaniki et al., 2018; Biasato et al., 2019). Studies have shown that replacing up to 15% of soybean or fishmeal with H. illucens meal in broiler and layer diets does not negatively affect productivity and often improves weight gain, feed conversion ratio, and meat and egg quality. Positive changes in gut microbiota composition and enhanced immune function have also been observed (Mwaniki et al., 2018).
In aquaculture, insect meal has been successfully used in feeds for tilapia, trout, catfish, carp, and shrimp, as it can replace up to 50% of fishmeal without compromising growth performance, with additional benefits for gut health and disease resistance (Henry et al., 2015; Van Huis, 2020).
Introducing 5%–10% insect meal into starter diets for piglets shows positive effects on growth, gastrointestinal health, and immunity. However, at levels above 15%, digestibility may decrease due to chitin content, indicating the need for additional processing steps (Biasato et al., 2019).
Different inclusion levels of H. illucens meal in animal diets affect productive and physiological parameters in various ways. Moderate levels (up to 10%–15%) generally lead to positive outcomes, whereas higher levels (>20%) may reduce digestibility, particularly in young animals, due to the higher chitin content (Table 9).
The final product’s effectiveness and safety depend on adherence to technological parameters and hygiene standards. Many European producers are implementing the HACCP and ISO 22000 standards in the insect processing stage.
