Nutritional advantages and application results of Hermetia illucens Larvae in feeding terrestrial and aquatic animals
Improving the efficiency of animal and aquaculture feeding has become a key objective in the context of global population growth and increasing demand for animal-derived products. A special focus is given to alternative protein sources, environmentally sustainable feed additives, and functional ingredients. Among these are insect proteins (Hermetia illucens), fermented products, algae, probiotics, and other innovative additives that demonstrate high nutritional value and positive effects on animal productivity.
High levels of crude protein, lipids, amino acids, minerals, and bioactive compounds characterize alternative feed additives (Table 10). The addition of insect protein and spirulina improves growth and egg quality (Tables 11 and 12).
Innovative feed ingredients (insect proteins, microalgae, probiotics, etc.) not only provide a high nutritional value but also significantly improve productivity and resilience in livestock and aquaculture species. These additives play a crucial role in the transition toward more sustainable, profitable, and environmentally friendly systems in animal husbandry and aquaculture. Nutritional value of Hermetia illucens Larvae meal and traditional feed ingredients
Fishmeal remains the leader in protein content, but Hermetia illucens (BSFL) offers a more environmentally friendly production process and
a well-balanced amino acid profile. Fishmeal may contain heavy metals and toxins, whereas soybean meal often contains pesticide residues (Weththasinghe et al., 2022). Properly processed BSFL meal poses minimal risks. Moreover, larvae cultivation contributes to organic waste recycling and requires significantly fewer resources than fish or soybean production (Fatima et al., 2023). While soybean and fishmeal are currently more affordable and well-established on the market, advancements in insect production technologies are making BSFL meal increasingly cost-competitive (Table 13).
Problems and Limitations in the Use of Hermetia illucens Products
