Insect-based feed production is relatively expensive due to high initial investments in equipment, climate control systems, and infrastructure for handling and storing organic materials (Van Huis and Oonincx, 2017). The lack of developed logistics for the collection and transport of organic waste is an additional burden. Moreover, the final cost of the product often exceeds that of conventional protein feed ingredients, such as soybean meal or fishmeal, hampering broader market acceptance. There is also limited understanding of the economic viability of such projects among investors and farmers, especially in countries with unstable economies or where governmental support is lacking (Delivering insect-based feed at scale. 2023). Regulatory and consumer perceptions
At the international level, no unified regulatory framework governing the use of insects in feed production exists. Within the European Union, insectbased feeds are approved only for certain animal categories and only when they are produced from safe substrates (EFSA, 2021a).
Environmental considerations
Although the bioconversion of organic waste using insects is considered an environmentally sustainable technology, certain ecological risks remain. Under intensive production conditions, greenhouse gas emissions—including methane and nitrous oxidemay occur, especially when moisture levels are imbalanced or substrates begin to decompose (Smetana et al., 2019a).
The management of residual frass—a by-product remaining after larval rearing—is another environmental challenge. Although frass has potential
as a biofertilizer, its properties and safety heavily depend on the initial substrate and processing methods. Without proper control, it may become contaminated with pathogens or heavy metals (Helen et al., 2024).
Future Potential and Research Directions
Innovations in bioconversion technologies
The advancement of bioconversion technology based on H. illucens offers promising opportunities for agriculture and related sectors. Current research and development efforts focus on optimizing production processes, expanding application areas, and enhancing system sustainability within the circular economy framework. The automation and digitalization of bioconversion processes is one of the key directions for future innovation. The use of sensors, environmental monitoring systems, and AI algorithms will increase the efficiency of waste-to-biomass conversion, minimize losses, and reduce production costs (Barragán-Fonseca et al., 2021).
Genetic selection and breeding of larvae with improved traits, such as faster growth rates, higher feed conversion efficiency, and resistance to pathogens or variable environmental conditions, is another promising area. Modern biotechnological tools, including genome editing and microbiome analysis, support a deeper understanding of larval physiology and improve it. Modern biotechnological approaches, including genome editing and metagenomic analysis of the microbiome, contribute to a deeper understanding of insect physiology and its potential for regulation (Xiong et al., 2024).
