The cultivation technology of H. illucens fits perfectly into the concept of the circular economy, where the waste of one industry becomes a resource for another. Examples include: utilization of food and agricultural waste, the return of frass as a biofertilizer for crop production, and the incorporation of the protein and lipid fractions of larvae into feed production chains and even pharmaceuticals (Surendra et al., 2016; Mina et al., 2023; Lu et al., 2025). Moreover, insect-rearing installations can be integrated into agro-industrial complexes, where bioconversion is a tool for processing internal organic waste streams (e.g., manure and feed residues) into economically valuable resources.
Application expansion across industries
Hermetia illucens biomass and its derived products can be applied not only in the feed industry but also in other promising sectors: bioplastics (e.g., based on chitin and proteins from larval exoskeletons) (VitalVilchis and Karunakaran, 2025); biofuels (larval fat can be converted into biodiesel) (Mohan et al. 2023); cosmetics and pharmaceuticals (larval extracts possess antibacterial and antioxidant properties) (Pimchan et al., 2024); and fertilizers and plant growth stimulators based on frass enriched with microbiota and amino acid residues (Fuhrmann et al., 2022).
Research gaps and future directions
Despite significant progress, substantial gaps remain in the scientific and practical foundation that require further exploration: long-term environmental impacts of large-scale insect farming (e.g., greenhouse gas emissions, effects on biodiversity), the safety and quality of insect-based products depending on the substrate types used, the optimization of nutritional value and bioavailability of proteins, lipids, and micronutrients for various species of farm animals and fish, the impact on animal health and productivity when feeds containing insect-derived ingredients are consumed, and the social perception and factors influencing the acceptance of this new technology by consumers and producers (Table 15). Table 15 presents an overview of the most promising bioconversion research directions using H. illucens. Key areas such as automation, genetics, integration into CES, and emerging applications such as bioplastics and biofuels are highlighted. These directions reflect the growing scientific and commercial interest in optimizing insectbased technologies’ sustainability, efficiency, and versatility.
Conclusion
BSF larvae (Hermetia illucens) offer a sustainable and efficient solution for converting diverse organic wastes into protein- and lipid-rich biomass suitable for animal feed, biofertilizers, and other applications. Their high substrate conversion rates and bioactive compound production highlight their potential for advancing circular economy approaches and sustainable protein production. Although progress has been made in optimizing rearing conditions and postharvest processing, challenges remain, including substrate standardization, microbial risk management, cost reduction, and production scaling up. Successful commercialization and broader adoption of BSFderived products will also depend on supportive regulatory frameworks and social acceptance. Overall, BSF bioconversion represents a promising technology for transforming waste management and animal nutrition, contingent on continued research, industry collaboration, and regulatory development.
